# [K70] `(?r)` (PCRE2's caseless-restrict: an ASCII character and a
# non-ASCII character must never match each other caselessly) — the fix
# for docs/dev/known_issues.md K70, found by lane ucpthink
# (`docs/dev/ucp_study.md` §G.1, 2026-09-28).
#
# WHAT THIS FILE COVERS.
#
#   1. BYTE (the default encoding): `(?r)` is a TRUE no-op, MEASURED against
#      libpcre2 10.46 both with and without it, across the ASCII fold pairs
#      and the whole Latin-1 range — `studies/k70_probe/probe_k70.py`,
#      transcript `studies/k70_probe/probe_k70_10.46.txt`. This is the
#      control that says the fix changes nothing under `byte`.
#   2. UTF8: `(?r)` REFUSES rather than silently cross the ASCII boundary it
#      names — the four witnesses `docs/dev/ucp_study.md` §G.1 found, plus
#      the KELVIN-pattern-vs-`k` direction. `perr` blocks assert only that
#      the pattern is rejected; the exact wording is pinned in
#      `tests/cli/run_cli_tests.sh`'s K70 block, which is the reject-table
#      shape this ENCODING-gated (rather than features-gated) refusal needs.
#   3. UTF8: `(?-r)` (the UNSET form) and the neighbouring `(?aD)` sub-letter
#      both STAY accepted — nothing ever persists an `r` state, and pcrec
#      has no UCP support at all, so the sub-letter has nothing to restrict
#      (K70's probe, UTF-alone-no-UCP rows).
#
# ORACLE: `(?r)` is a PCRE2-only construct (python's `re` module has no such
# syntax) — every `(?r)`-bearing block below is `# pcre2-only`. Every cell
# was read from libpcre2 10.46 before being written (transcripts above),
# not reasoned out.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. byte: (?r) is a true no-op (control)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# pcre2-only
pattern (?i)(?r)a
encoding byte
m "A" 0 1
m "a" 0 1

# The Latin-1 pair: bytes >= 0x80 never fold under `byte` (D23), so `(?r)`
# changes nothing here either — both directions nomatch, with and without it.
# pcre2-only
pattern (?i)(?r)\xe9
encoding byte
n "\xc9"
m "\xe9" 0 1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. utf8: (?r) refuses — the fix
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# pcre2-only
pattern (?i)(?r)k
encoding utf8
perr

# pcre2-only
pattern (?i)(?r)s
encoding utf8
perr

# pcre2-only
pattern (?i)(?r)[a-z]
encoding utf8
perr

# pcre2-only
pattern (?i)(?r)\x{212a}
encoding utf8
perr

# `(?r)` before `(?i)` — the order the option run is spelled must not matter,
# it is still an unhyphenated `r` under utf8.
# pcre2-only
pattern (?r)(?i)k
encoding utf8
perr

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. utf8: what stays accepted
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# (?-r) alone: never preceded by a successful (?r) at this encoding, so
# there is no state to unset — a genuine no-op, not a refusal.
# pcre2-only
pattern (?-r)k
encoding utf8
m "k" 0 1
n "K"

# (?aD): the ASCII-restrict sub-letter has nothing to restrict — pcrec
# implements no UCP, so \d is already ASCII-only under utf8 regardless.
# `(?aD)` is PCRE2-only syntax (python's re has no such inline group).
# pcre2-only
pattern (?aD)\d
encoding utf8
m "3" 0 1
n "\xd9\xa3"
